# Chroma content pack, Politics, Elections and Public Office (modern Earth first, with the tribal and magic worlds). PART 3
# of the pack's moments: SIDE ROADS AND COMMUNITY RUNGS, an everyday pair per title that only its holder meets (holds:): the
# constituency caseworker, the party official, the lobbyist, the policy analyst, the speechwriter, the pollster, and the three
# community rungs (campaign volunteer, polling-station volunteer, local party officer).
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md. Brief: chroma-packs/politics/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md
# (part 3). Titles and perks: chroma-packs/politics/catalogue.py, chroma-packs/core/reach.py and the base catalogue; the form of
# each title and perk in the tribal and magic worlds: chroma-packs/politics/worlds.py.
# Stages follow each title's catalogue ages against the engine's stage entry windows (juvenile 10-15, young_adult 15-25, adult
# 24-36, mature 40-60, elder 60-80), so every holder can meet its pair: a holder of 18 to 25 may still be juvenile, and one past
# 60 may be elder; a campaign volunteer of 14 may still be a child. Holder pairs take no new title (engine, after its Science
# test): an option that points at the next rung carries aims: instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- constituency caseworker: calls BR / WUG (cycle s4)

== a family about to lose their home
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per constituency caseworker a year: a housing case most weeks, an eviction at short notice every month or two; about 1 life in 1,000 works as a caseworker (catalogue share; estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-75
calls: BR
holds: constituency caseworker
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague
earth: an eviction notice for Monday lands on your desk, and the family it names is waiting in the corridor
tribal: the band means to drive a family out over a feud before the next move of camp, and they come to you to carry their case to the speaker
magic: the guild that owns their tenement will throw a family out at the week's end, and they bring their petition to your desk
scene: A family of four waits in the corridor of the member's office with a bailiff's letter: out on Monday, three days from now. {N} has the file open and the phone already in hand.
scene W: The council has a legal duty to a family made homeless, and {N} knows the paragraph by heart. Someone only has to be made to follow it.
scene U: The notice is dated oddly, and the landlord's paperwork looks thin. {N} reads it twice, looking for the flaw that buys time.
scene B: The landlord owns half the street and has never once returned the office's calls. A win here would be noticed, and {N} knows how to make it loud.
scene R: The father keeps apologising for taking up time. {N} feels the anger rise: three days is nothing, and nobody else in the building is going to fight for them.
scene G: The family have lived on that street for twenty years, and {N} grew up three streets away. Everyone there knows them by name.
- W | open a homelessness case with the council, and hold it to its legal duty | v: universalism, conformity | grants: constituency casework | tribal: ask the elders to hold the band to its custom of sheltering a family in trouble | magic: open a case with the Crown's almoner, and hold the city to its charter | chance: 75
- U | comb the notice for the legal flaw that buys the family time | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | tribal: ask the old ones whether the feud was ever settled by custom, and hold the band to that | magic: comb the guild's writ for a flaw in its seals that buys the family time | chance: 55
- B | ring the landlord and hint that the member can slow his next planning application | v: power | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: an office's power used as a threat; backfire: the landlord records the call and sends it to the local paper | tribal: warn the feuding family's head that the speaker will remember who drove them out | magic: let the guild's steward know that the member sits on the licensing board | chance: 65
- R | go round to the landlord's office and argue it out face to face | v: benevolence | identity | tribal: walk to the feuding family's hearth and argue it out face to face | magic: march to the guild hall and argue it out with the steward face to face | chance: 40
- G | find the family a room with relatives or neighbours until something better comes | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: find the family a place at a kinsman's hearth until the quarrel cools | chance: 80
- W>G | use the council's own rules to get them rehoused in the same neighbourhood | v: security, conformity | tribal: invoke the custom that a family keeps its place in the camp it was born to | magic: invoke the ward's old charter, so the family is housed in its own parish | chance: 60
- U>W | draft the member's letter so precisely that the council cannot wriggle out of its duty | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: frame the family's case so exactly that the speaker cannot let it drop | magic: draft the member's sealed letter so precisely that the city cannot evade its duty | chance: 70
- B>U | trade a favour with a friendly housing officer for the landlord's file | v: power, achievement | tribal: trade a gift with the elder who knows the whole story of the feud | magic: trade a favour with a guild clerk for the tenement's ledger | chance: 75
- R>B | tip off the local paper, and make the landlord the story by Sunday | v: power, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: going to the press without the member's leave; backfire: the member is furious, and the next big case goes to a colleague | tribal: ask the singers to make a song of the family's wrong before the gathering | magic: carry the story to the broadsheet printers in the market square | chance: 65
- G>R | gather the neighbours on the family's doorstep for Monday morning | v: benevolence, universalism | binds | identity | tribal: gather the neighbouring hearths to stand around the family's shelter | magic: gather the lane's neighbours on the tenement steps at dawn | chance: 35
works: The family has a roof before the deadline, close to the street they know.
works: The people driving the family out give them a month's grace, and by the end of it a new place is found.
fails: The deadline comes, and the family's things are carried out to a friend's shed.
fails: Nobody with the power to stop it will listen, and {N} spends the days knocking on doors that stay shut.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
== the same case for the third time
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per constituency caseworker: most months (estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-75
calls: WUG
holds: constituency caseworker
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the same name is back in your inbox, with the same complaint, for the third time this year
tribal: the same widow sits at your fire with the same grievance about her share of the hunt, for the third time this season
magic: the same petition is back on your desk, under a new seal, with the same complaint inside
scene: The widow from the estate is back: her payment has been stopped again by the same error, the third time this year. She brings a tin of biscuits, and she knows {Ns} name.
scene W: Each time, {N} fixes it by the book, and each time the book sends it back. A rule that fails the same woman three times is a broken rule.
scene U: The same code on the same letter, three times over. {N} begins to suspect the fault is not in her file at all, but in the system that reads it.
scene B: The office is judged on cases closed, and this one keeps coming back open. {colleague} has started passing her to {N} on purpose.
scene R: {N} feels a flash of impatience at the sight of her name, and then a flush of shame at the flash.
scene G: She has had nobody much to talk to since her husband died, and these visits are part of her month. {N} has started keeping the good chair free for her.
- W | fix it by the book again, and log every step in her file | v: conformity, benevolence | habit | self_control: + | tribal: put her share right by custom again, and tell the elders each step | magic: put it right by the rules again, and enter every step in her file | chance: 92
- U | trace the error to its source in the benefits system, and report the fault | v: achievement, universalism | mark: learned a skill | tribal: trace the quarrel back to the first share that was cut wrong | magic: trace the error back to the clerk's ledger where it began | chance: 45
- B | pass her to the newest colleague, and close the file on your own list | v: power | mark: refused someone in need | self_control: - | tribal: send her to the youngest of the speaker's helpers, and be free of it | magic: pass her to the newest clerk, and strike her from your own roll | chance: 95
- R | tell her straight that this is the last time the office can do it for her | v: self-direction | tribal: tell her straight that this is the last time you will carry it for her | magic: tell her straight that this is the last petition the office will carry for her | chance: 65
- G | put the kettle on and fix it again, as part of the month | v: benevolence, tradition | habit | self_control: + | grants: constituency casework | tribal: sit with her by the fire and put it right again, as part of the season | magic: pour her a cup and put it right again, as part of the month | chance: 92
- W>G | set up a standing arrangement with the benefits office for her and others like her | v: benevolence, security | binds | grants: handling red tape | tribal: get the hunt leader to set her share aside each time, without her having to ask | magic: get the almoner's office to set her dole aside each quarter, without a fresh petition | chance: 55
- U>W | gather every case with the same error into one complaint to the ombudsman | v: universalism | identity | tribal: gather every family wronged the same way, and bring them before the elders together | magic: gather every petition with the same flaw into one plea to the master of rolls | chance: 30
- B>U | get the member to put a written question to the minister and demand the figures | v: power, achievement | tribal: have the speaker ask at the gathering, before every band, how the shares are counted | magic: have the member lay a sealed question before the Assembly and demand the ledgers | chance: 70
- R>B | lose your temper with the benefits office until a manager takes the case | v: power, stimulation | self_control: - | tribal: storm over to the hunt leader's fire and shout until he puts it right | magic: storm into the almoner's office and shout until the master comes out | chance: 65
- G>R | take her along to the lunch club at the community centre, so the month holds more | v: benevolence, hedonism | binds | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: bring her to sit with the old women at the evening fire | magic: bring her to the widows' table at the tavern by the gate | chance: 75
works: The fault is found at last, and the money arrives on time three months running.
works: She comes by to say it is sorted and stays to talk a while, and {N} does not mind at all.
fails: The same error comes back in the spring, word for word.
fails: {boss} asks why one person takes up so much of the office's week, and {N} has no good answer.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- party official: calls WB / URG (cycle s1)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== the leadership wants its favourite selected
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per party official a year: about 1 in 5 meet a selection the centre wants to steer; about 1 life in 2,000 works for a party (catalogue share; estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-75
calls: WB
holds: party official
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, elder
earth: an email from headquarters asks for the leadership's favourite to be chosen for the seat, and the rules say the local members choose
tribal: the faction's head wants his nephew sent to the gathering, and custom says the families choose their own speaker
magic: the faction's lord wants his favourite on the lot for the seat, and the faction's charter says the sworn members choose
scene: The email from headquarters is three lines long: the leadership would be grateful if the selection for the seat went to its favourite. The rules {N} keeps say the local members choose, by open ballot.
scene W: {N} has run forty selections by the rulebook, and the rulebook is the one thing every member trusts. Bend it once, and every loser after this will say it was bent again.
scene U: {N} reads the rules again, slowly. Three clauses would let the centre shape the shortlist without breaking a single one.
scene B: {boss} sent the email, and {boss} decides who runs the region next year. The favourite will probably win anyway; the only question is what helping is worth.
scene R: The members have a candidate of their own, a nurse from the ward who has knocked on doors for ten years. {N} feels sick at the thought of telling her the race was over before it began.
scene G: The branch has picked its own candidates for as long as anyone remembers, and {elder} still talks about the one time the centre sent someone in.
- W | run the selection by the book, open ballot and all, and tell headquarters so | v: conformity, universalism | identity | mark: defied an authority | tribal: hold the choosing by custom, every family heard, and tell the faction head so | magic: hold the choosing by the charter, every sworn lot counted, and tell the lord so | chance: 75
- U | find the clause that lets the centre shape the shortlist without breaking a rule | v: achievement, power | tribal: find the old custom that lets a faction head name who may be chosen | magic: find the clause in the charter that lets the lord shape the lot | chance: 70
- B | ask headquarters, plainly, what delivering the favourite would be worth | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask the faction head, plainly, what helping his nephew would be worth | magic: ask the lord's steward, plainly, what the favour would be worth | chance: 70
- R | ring the members' own candidate tonight and tell her about the email | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: defied an authority | tribal: go to the families' own choice tonight and tell her what the faction head wants | magic: send word tonight to the members' own choice about the lord's letter | chance: 95
- G | let the branch elders know quietly, and leave them to settle it among themselves | v: tradition | tribal: tell the faction's old women quietly, and leave them to settle it | magic: let the ward's oldest members know quietly, and leave them to settle it | chance: 60
- W>U | ask the party's rules committee for a written ruling before doing anything | v: conformity | tribal: ask the oldest keeper of custom how such a choosing was made before | magic: ask the faction's keeper of the charter for a ruling in writing | chance: 65
- U>B | brief headquarters on which branch votes the favourite needs, and how to win them | v: power, achievement | grants: a name as a fixer | tribal: tell the faction head which families his nephew must win, and how | magic: tell the lord's steward which members the favourite must win, and how | chance: 60
- B>R | strike a deal: the favourite takes this seat, the members' candidate the next | v: power, benevolence | binds | tribal: strike a bargain: the nephew goes this summer, the families' choice the next | magic: strike a bargain: the favourite takes this lot, the members' choice the next | chance: 35
- R>G | read the email aloud at the branch meeting, and let the members decide whether to fight | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: tell the whole camp at the fire what the faction head wants, and let the families decide | magic: read the lord's letter aloud at the ward meeting, and let the members decide | chance: 70
- G>W | shortlist the favourite alone, as the branch always did when the centre asked | v: tradition, conformity | mark: gave in to pressure | closed: approval: a selection fixed against the party's own rules; backfire: a member leaks the emails, and the result is overturned on appeal | tribal: put only the nephew forward, as the families always did when a faction head asked | magic: put only the favourite on the lot, as the ward always did when the lord asked | chance: 80
works: The selection goes ahead, and the members accept the result, even those who lost.
works: Those at the top let the matter drop, and nobody mentions the request again.
fails: The losing side cries foul, and the choice has to be made all over again.
fails: {boss} stops asking {N} into the rooms where things are decided, and the reason is plain enough.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== the party is broke after the election
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per party official: after most lost elections, about one year in four, money and staff are cut (estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-75
calls: URG
holds: party official
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, elder
earth: after the lost election comes the meeting about the overdraft: three months of money left and twelve staff on the payroll
tribal: the faction's stores are gone after the feasting, the cold moons are coming, and the families who gave are tired of giving
magic: the faction's coffer is empty after the lots, and the clerks and heralds still want paying
scene: The election is lost, and so is the money. The meeting about the overdraft ends with one line on the whiteboard: three months left, twelve people on the payroll, and {N} has to say who goes.
scene W: Every person on that payroll was promised a job through the next campaign, and {N} signed half of those letters.
scene U: {N} has the figures open: membership fees, small donors, the big ones who went quiet. There is a pattern in who stopped giving, and when.
scene B: Two big donors have said they will come back if the party changes course. One of them has {Ns} private number.
scene R: {colleague} has a baby due in March and is on the list. {N} wants to tear the list up and find the money anywhere at all.
scene G: The party has been broke before, after the last landslide and the one before it. {elder}, the old treasurer, says it always comes back, slowly, from the bottom up.
- W | cut every post by the same share, so nobody carries more than anyone else | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: cut every family's share of what is left by the same handful | magic: cut every clerk's wage by the same share, so nobody carries more | chance: 75
- U | work out who stopped giving and why, and write to each of them | v: achievement | grants: fundraising | tribal: work out which families stopped giving, and why, and go to each | chance: 55
- B | take the big donor's money, and the strings that come with it | v: power | mark: gave in to pressure | tribal: accept the rich family's gifts, and the say over the faction that comes with them | magic: take the merchant prince's gold, and the strings that come with it | chance: 90
- R | give up your own salary for a quarter to keep the youngest staff on | v: benevolence | identity | binds | self_control: + | tribal: give your own share of the stores to keep the youngest helpers fed | magic: give up your own wage for a season to keep the youngest clerks on | chance: 92
- G | close the regional offices, and run the party from members' front rooms | v: tradition | identity | tribal: break the faction's big camp into family hearths until the herds come back | magic: give up the faction's town house, and meet in members' kitchens | chance: 75
- W>U | bring in the auditors, and publish every account to the members | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: lay out every store and every gift before the faction's elders | magic: open the coffer's ledgers to every sworn member | chance: 75
- U>B | sell the party's membership lists to a marketing firm | v: power, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: selling members' personal data without their consent; backfire: a regulator's fine, and the story in every paper | self_control: - | tribal: trade the faction's secrets about who owes whom to a rival clan's go-between | magic: sell the faction's roll of members to a merchant house | chance: 80
- B>R | stage a gala dinner with a famous speaker, and charge what the room can bear | v: achievement, hedonism | grants: fundraising | tribal: hold a great feast with the best singer in the valley, and ask every guest for a gift | magic: stage a masked ball at the guild hall with a famous bard, and sell the seats dear | chance: 65
- R>G | ask every member yourself, one by one, for a small gift each month | v: benevolence, universalism | habit | self_control: + | tribal: walk to every hearth yourself and ask each for a little | magic: knock on every member's door yourself and ask for a few coins each month | chance: 60
- G>W | ask the old treasurer how the party came back last time, and follow the plan | v: tradition, security | tribal: ask the oldest keeper of the stores how the faction came through the last lean winter, and do the same | chance: 70
works: By spring the books balance, and nobody leaves who did not choose to.
works: The members come through with small sums, and the doors stay open.
fails: The money runs out first, and four people are let go in a single afternoon.
fails: The money comes in, and with it a donor who expects a say in everything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- lobbyist: calls UR / WBG (cycle s2)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
== a client wants the minister by Friday
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per lobbyist a year: several times; about 1 life in 500 lobbies for a living (catalogue share; OpenSecrets: about 12,000 to 13,000 registered US federal lobbyists a year; estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-80
calls: UR
holds: lobbyist
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: week
roles: friend, boss, colleague
earth: a client on the phone wants a meeting with the minister by Friday, before the decision on its routes
tribal: the flint-traders want the chief's ear before the gathering ends, and they have brought gifts for whoever can get it
magic: a merchant house wants an audience with the Keeper of the Treasury by the week's end, and it pays in gold
scene: The client runs the region's buses, and a new rule on country routes will be decided next week. It wants twenty minutes with the minister by Friday, and it is paying {N} for exactly that.
scene W: There is a proper way to do this: a registered meeting, an agenda, a line in the minister's diary for anyone to read. {N} has always done it that way.
scene U: {N} knows which official actually drafts the rule, and that official reads evidence, not phone calls. One good paper may matter more than twenty minutes of a minister.
scene B: {friend}, an old colleague, now works in the minister's private office. One call would do it, and everyone in the trade knows it.
scene R: Four days and one shot at it: this is the part of the job that still makes {Ns} pulse race.
scene G: The buses run through villages {N} grew up near. The client is not wrong that the new rule would cut the last route out to them.
- W | request the meeting through the official channel, entered in the register | v: conformity | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: ask leave to speak at the chief's fire, holding up the go-between's token | magic: petition for an audience through the Keeper's own clerks, entered in the court's book | chance: 20
- U | send the official who drafts the rule a short paper with the route figures | v: achievement, universalism | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: tell the old hand who carries out the chief's word how the trade paths would be cut | magic: send the Keeper's clerk a short treatise with the road figures | chance: 65
- B | ring your old colleague in the private office, and ask for twenty minutes as a favour | v: power | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: ask your friend at the chief's side for a word in his ear before the fire | magic: send a note to your old fellow clerk at the Keeper's side, and ask for the audience as a favour | chance: 75
- R | wait at the minister's Friday surgery, and make the case in person | v: stimulation, self-direction | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: wait by the path the chief walks at dawn, and make the case to his face | magic: wait on the Keeper's stair at the morning bell, and make the case in person | chance: 60
- G | bring the village riders to speak, and say nothing of who paid their fares | v: power, universalism | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a client's campaign passed off as a local one; backfire: a reporter finds out who paid the fares | tribal: bring the families of the trade path to speak, and never say the traders sent them | magic: bring the villagers to speak for the road, and never say the merchant house paid their way | chance: 60
- W>B | tell the client plainly what the rules allow by Friday, and bill for the advice | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: tell the traders plainly what custom allows before the gathering ends, and take a fair gift for it | magic: tell the merchant house plainly what the court's rules allow, and send the bill | chance: 75
- U>R | write a sharp piece for the morning papers that puts the village route on every table | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: give the singers a sharp verse about the cut path, to sing at every fire | magic: write a sharp broadsheet that puts the village road in every tavern | chance: 40
- B>G | make the client promise to keep the village routes, as the price of your help | v: power, benevolence | binds | tribal: make the traders promise to keep the old path open, as the price of your help | magic: make the merchant house promise to keep the village road, as the price of your help | chance: 60
- R>W | argue with the client's board until it agrees to make its case in public | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: argue with the traders until they agree to plead openly at the fire | magic: argue with the merchant house's elders until they agree to plead openly in the Assembly | chance: 55
- G>U | ask the parish councils and the drivers who rides the route, and when | v: universalism, tradition | tribal: ask the families along the trade path how often they walk it, and for what | magic: ask the carters' guild and the village elders who rides the road, and how often | chance: 90
works: The case is heard where it counts before the rule is decided, and the client signs on for another year.
works: The client grumbles at first, then agrees that {N} called it right.
fails: The week ends with nothing to show, and the client takes its business to a rival.
fails: Word of how {N} went about it goes round the corridors, and two doors close.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 4 options (check.py section 4).
== an old colleague now decides
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per lobbyist a year: about 1 in 5 find a former colleague in a post that decides a client's case, most often after a change of government (estimate)
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-80
calls: WBG
holds: lobbyist
tone: mixed
life: work, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, colleague, boss
earth: a former colleague's name is in the new appointments, and the friend you shared an office with now decides your client's case
tribal: the hunter you grew up with is now the chief's speaker, and the flint-traders you plead for want you at his fire tomorrow
magic: your old fellow clerk is now a councillor of the Crown, and the merchant house you serve wants you at his door by morning
scene: The new appointments are out, and {friend}, who shared {Ns} old office for six years, now signs off the very decision {Ns} biggest client is waiting on. A message arrives within the hour: drinks soon, like old times?
scene W: There are rules for exactly this: declare the friendship, keep every contact on the record, let someone else handle the client if need be.
scene U: {N} knows how {friend} thinks, which papers {friend} reads first, which arguments land. That knowledge is worth more than any favour, and it is perfectly legal.
scene B: In this trade a friend in the right chair is the whole game. The client knows it too; it hired {N} partly for the address book.
scene R: {N} likes {friend}, truly likes them, and the thought of turning the friendship into a sales call sits badly.
scene G: Six years of shared lunches and leaving cards. {N} would rather keep the friend than win the case.
- W | declare the friendship to your firm, and hand the client to a colleague | v: conformity | identity | self_control: + | tribal: tell the traders of the old bond, and send another go-between to the fire | magic: declare the old friendship to the house, and send another advocate to the councillor | chance: 92
- U | write a case that would win on any desk, and file it the usual way | v: achievement | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: set the traders' case out so plainly that any speaker would take it, and bring it the usual way | magic: write a petition so sound it would win at any desk, and file it through the usual door | chance: 55
- B | go for the drinks, and make the case over the second glass | v: power, hedonism | habit | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lobbying an official off the record; backfire: a photograph of the two of them turns up in a gossip column | self_control: - | requires: registered lobbyist | without: law | tribal: share a meal at his fire, and make the case over the meat | magic: share a bottle at the tavern by the councillor's gate, and make the case over the second cup | chance: 70
- R | go for the drinks, enjoy the evening, and never once mention work | v: hedonism, benevolence | tribal: sit at his fire for the night, laugh about the old hunts, and never mention the traders | magic: drink with him at the old tavern, and never once mention the house | chance: 95
- G | stay away until the case is decided, and pick the friendship up after | v: tradition | self_control: + | tribal: keep away from his fire until the gathering decides, and sit with him after | chance: 90
- W>B | ask the ethics office for a ruling, then work the case inside it | v: conformity | tribal: ask the elders what custom allows between old friends, then plead inside it | magic: ask the court's warden of conduct for a ruling, then work the case inside it | chance: 70
- U>R | build a clean wall between yourself and the case, and see your friend freely | v: self-direction, benevolence | binds | tribal: let another go-between carry the traders' gifts, so you can sit at his fire freely | magic: wall yourself off from the house's petition, so you can see your friend freely | chance: 75
- B>G | trade stepping back from the case for a long, steady contract with your firm | v: power | tribal: trade stepping back for a steady share of flint from the traders each season | magic: trade stepping back for a long, steady retainer from the house | chance: 55
- R>W | tell the client to its face that the friendship is not for sale | v: stimulation, universalism | mark: made an enemy | tribal: tell the traders to their faces that the old bond is not for trade | magic: tell the merchant house to its face that the friendship is not for sale | chance: 65
- G>U | wait a season, watch how your friend runs the new post, and learn its ways | v: tradition, self-direction | tribal: wait a moon, watch how he keeps the new duty, and learn his ways | chance: 70
works: The case is decided on its merits, and the friendship comes through intact.
works: The client stays, and {friend} says later that {N} handled it exactly right.
fails: The client moves its business to a firm with fewer scruples, or better friends.
